{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"07","chapter_title":"Course Completion and Transfer","title":"Final Submission Checklist","description":"CAT 100 textbook section on final submission checks, centered on a final submission checklist, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Translate each literal assignment requirement into a testable checklist row with a pass condition.","Verify each row from the recipient's position, such as loading links in a signed-out window and opening the exported file you actually submitted.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer","cat100_supplement_final_submission_checklist","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/08/06"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_8_7_final_submission_checklist_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_8_7_final_submission_checklist_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_8_7_final_submission_checklist_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_8_7_final_submission_checklist_calibration"]},"content":"# Final Submission Checklist\n\n![Final Submission Checklist textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/08-07-final-submission-checklist.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMore submissions lose points to logistics than to weak content. A deck submitted in the wrong format, a portfolio link that returns a 404 to anyone not signed into your account, a file named `final.pdf` that the grader cannot tell apart from forty others: each of these is fully avoidable and each can sink an otherwise strong artifact. The final submission checklist is the last gate, and its whole purpose is to replace the feeling that the work is done with proof that it is deliverable.\n\nThe deeper habit here is verification from the recipient's position. You know your link works because it works for you, signed in, on your machine. The grader is none of those things. A checklist forces you to open the exported file yourself, click the link in an incognito window, and read the assignment requirements literally. It is the difference between \"I think I submitted correctly\" and \"I confirmed each requirement with a test.\"\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Translate each literal assignment requirement into a testable checklist row with a pass condition.\n- Verify each row from the recipient's position, such as loading links in a signed-out window and opening the exported file you actually submitted.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nAlex finishes a capstone with three deliverables and uploads them. The assignment said, \"Submit slides as a PDF and include a public link to your site.\" Alex uploaded the PowerPoint file, not a PDF, and pasted a GitHub Pages link that happens to load only because Alex is signed into the account that owns the repository.\n\nTo Alex, everything looks submitted and working. To the grader, the slides may render with shifted fonts in a different PowerPoint version, and the site link returns a 404 because the grader is not Alex. The work is strong; the submission is broken. The checklist exists to catch exactly this gap between \"works for me\" and \"works for the person receiving it.\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA submission checklist is a list of concrete, testable requirements, where each item is verified by an action you actually perform, not by recollection. The items fall into a few categories that recur across almost every assignment:\n\n| Category | Item | The test that verifies it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Format | Correct file type (PDF, not PPTX) | Export, then open the exported file and confirm nothing shifted |\n| Access | Link is public | Open it in an incognito or signed-out window and confirm no 404 |\n\nA signed-out 404 can also mean the GitHub Pages build has not finished yet (Pages publishes via a build workflow and can lag a minute or two after a push). Wait for the build to report success under the repo's Actions tab or Pages settings, then re-run the incognito test; the live URL is the source of truth.\n| Identification | Filename follows the convention | Read the requirement, rename to match (`lastname_course_v3.pdf`) |\n| Safety | No private data or credentials | For a repo artifact, rerun the `git grep` credential scan; for a document or spreadsheet, open the file and scan for embedded keys, passwords, tokens, or personal data |\n| Correctness | No broken values | Open the file and confirm totals compute, with no `#REF!` |\n\nThe unifying principle is testing from the recipient's environment. Exporting to PDF is not enough; you open the PDF to confirm a chart did not move. Pasting a link is not enough; you load it while signed out, which is the single most common catch, because GitHub Pages, Google Drive, and many tools quietly grant you access they do not grant a stranger. Each checklist row is a hypothesis (\"the link is public\") paired with the experiment that confirms it (\"loaded it incognito, no 404\").\n\nThe same recipient-position logic bounds AI help. As of 2026, a chat assistant such as ChatGPT or Claude can draft the checklist rows for you, but it cannot reliably open your exported PDF or load your link the way the grader will: file handling is gated by format and plan, and even an assistant that can fetch a URL does so through its own service, not through a signed-out, no-account browser session. The verification tests stay yours to run.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your portfolio link loads fine when you click it. Is the public-link requirement satisfied?\" explanation=\"It loads for you because you are signed into the account that owns it. Signed-in access is invisible privilege; the only honest test is loading it in an incognito or signed-out window and confirming no 404.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes; if it loads for you, it loads for the grader.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No; you must load it signed out in an incognito window to confirm no 404.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, as long as the URL contains the word public.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Running the Final Submission Checklist\n\nA student is ready to submit a capstone with three deliverables: a slide deck, a portfolio link, and a budget spreadsheet. The assignment says, \"Submit slides as a PDF and include a public link to your site.\" They run each checklist item and record the result.\n\n| Checklist item | Action and result | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Correct export format | Exported `presentation.pptx` to PDF; opened the PDF and confirmed fonts and chart did not shift | Pass |\n| Link is public | Opened the GitHub Pages link in an incognito window while signed out; page loaded, no 404 | Pass |\n| Filename convention | Renamed `final.pdf` to `moon_cat100_capstone_v3.pdf` so it is identifiable | Pass |\n| No private data | Reran `git grep -i \"key\\|password\"` on the repo (zero hits); opened the PDF deck and the budget spreadsheet and scanned them for embedded keys, tokens, and personal data (none found) | Pass |\n| Spreadsheet totals | Confirmed Total cell B14 reads `=SUM(B2:B13)` = 8,420 with no `#REF!` | Pass |\n\nThe checklist is not cosmetic. Each row is a concrete test the student actually performed, including opening the exported file and the link from a signed-out browser. The submission goes out only after every item shows a verifiable pass, not after the work merely \"feels done.\"\n\nRun the checklist as a fixed procedure. Predict each step before revealing it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Verify a submission from the recipient’s position\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The assignment says submit slides as a PDF. What is the first move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Upload the PowerPoint file; it is basically the same\",\"Read the requirement literally and export to PDF\",\"Email the grader to ask if PPTX is fine\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Read the requirement literally: slides as a PDF means a PDF, not the source file. Convert the exact format the assignment states.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Open what you exported. Conversion routinely shifts charts and fonts, so confirm fidelity by opening the PDF, not by trusting the export button.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Test access while signed out. Load every link in an incognito window; a 404 there is a 404 for the grader, the most common catch.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finish the safety and correctness rows: rename to the convention, re-scan for credentials with zero hits, and confirm the total computes. Submit only when every row passes.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the submission-verification steps:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Read each requirement literally and turn it into a testable row\",\"Export to the required format, then open the exported file to confirm fidelity\",\"Load every link signed out in an incognito window\",\"Rename the file to the stated convention and re-scan for credentials\",\"Submit only after every row passes its test\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThese four concepts anchor the section; trace how they connect:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer\",\"cat100_supplement_final_submission_checklist\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nSubmit only after every requirement has passed a test you ran from the recipient's position. Before you click submit:\n\n1. **Read the requirement literally.** \"Slides as a PDF\" means a PDF, not the source file. Match the exact format and naming the assignment states.\n2. **Open what you exported.** A PDF that lost a chart is worse than the original; confirm the file by opening it, not by trusting the export button.\n3. **Test access while signed out.** Load every link in an incognito window. If it 404s there, it 404s for the grader.\n\nIf any item cannot be tested to a pass, the artifact is not ready to submit, however finished it feels.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is trusting that a link works because it works for you. Signed-in access is invisible privilege; the incognito test is the only honest check. Skipping it is the most common reason strong portfolios receive a zero for the link.\n\nThe second misreading is treating the checklist as bureaucratic busywork. Each row prevents a specific, real failure: wrong format, broken access, an unidentifiable filename, a leaked credential, a broken total. The checklist is not ceremony; it is the set of mistakes that actually cost students points, written down so you stop repeating them.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nBegin by reading the assignment instructions and converting each requirement into a testable row. \"Submit slides as a PDF\" becomes two rows: export to PDF, and open the PDF to confirm fidelity. \"Include a public link\" becomes one row with an explicit incognito test. Turning prose requirements into tests is the move that prevents \"I thought it said\" mistakes.\n\nWork the rows with real actions. Export the deck, then open the resulting PDF and look at the chart and fonts, because conversion routinely shifts them. Copy the site link, open a private window, sign out, and load it; a 404 here is the failure you most want to catch before the grader does. Rename the file to the stated convention so it is identifiable among many. Rerun the credential scan against whatever artifact actually carries text or data, not only the repo: `git grep` covers tracked files in a git repository and does nothing for a standalone PDF or spreadsheet, so open those files and scan for embedded API keys, passwords, tokens, or personal data (a leftover key in a worksheet cell or in the speaker notes is the classic miss). Then open the spreadsheet to confirm the total computes.\n\nMark each row pass only after its test passes, and submit only when the whole table is green. The walkthrough output is a checklist table where every status is backed by an action you can describe, not a feeling.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, write the test, not just the item, for each requirement of an assignment you actually have. A strong reader does not list \"link is public\"; they write \"open it incognito, expect no 404,\" because the test is what makes the item verifiable.\n\nA strong reader of the final submission checklist can answer four questions:\n\n1. What does the assignment require, stated literally, and is each requirement a row?\n2. For each row, what action verified it, performed from the recipient's environment?\n3. Which link or file did you test while signed out, and what did you see?\n4. Did anything fail a test, and was submission held until it passed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe submission is strong when every requirement maps to a row, every row records the action that verified it, and access items were tested signed out. The standard is a checklist where each status is justified by a test, not by the sense that the work is finished.\n\nA useful closing note would read: \"I converted each assignment requirement into a testable row. I exported the deck to PDF and opened it to confirm the chart did not shift, loaded the site link in an incognito window and saw no 404, renamed the file to `moon_cat100_capstone_v3.pdf`, reran the credential scan with zero hits, and confirmed the spreadsheet total computes. I submitted only after every row passed its test.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nApply the same checklist discipline to a job application, where the recipient is a recruiter and the requirements live in the posting. \"Attach your resume as a PDF\" and \"include a link to your portfolio\" become the same testable rows, but the cost of a failed access test is now a missed opportunity rather than lost points. Loading your own portfolio link from a friend's signed-out browser before you send the application is the test of whether you carry submission verification into high-stakes settings, not just into the course where you first learned it.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the final submission checklist packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/08-07-final-submission-checklist-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"final submission checklist\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"08.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the final submission checklist.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the final submission checklist for CAT 100 08.07. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_final_submission_checklist\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_final_submission_checklist_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_final_submission_checklist","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_final_submission_checklist","cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer"],"stem":"The assignment says: 'Submit your slides as a PDF.' You upload presentation.pptx because it is your working file. What does the final checklist flag?","options":["Nothing; the content is the same either way.","Wrong export format; convert to PDF and open the PDF once to confirm the layout and fonts survived before submitting.","The file should be a .docx instead.","You should email the slides rather than upload them."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A PDF preserves layout and is the required type. The checklist step is to export and then verify the exported file looks right.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_wrong_export_format","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_final_submission_checklist_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You submit your GitHub Pages link. It loads fine in your browser, where you are signed in. What is the missing verification step?","options":["Nothing; if it loads for you it loads for everyone.","Open the link in an incognito or signed-out window to confirm it is public and not a 404 or private page for the grader.","Email the link to yourself first.","Add the link twice in case one fails."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A link that works while signed in may be private or local. Testing in a signed-out window confirms the grader can actually reach it.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_untested_link","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_final_submission_checklist_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"A chat assistant such as ChatGPT or Claude offers to auto-generate your final checklist and confirm everything is done. How should you use it within a safe boundary?","options":["Let it confirm the submission is complete and submit based on that.","Let it propose checklist items, but verify each one yourself by opening the file, testing the link, and checking the format before you submit.","Avoid checklists entirely.","Have it submit the assignment for you."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The AI can draft the checklist, but the learner verifies each item against the real artifact. The tool cannot confirm your file opens or your link is public; even an assistant that can fetch a URL does so through its own service, not the grader's signed-out session.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_ai_confirms_complete","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_final_submission_checklist_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which filename best follows a clear submission convention?","options":["final.docx","moon_cat100_capstone_v3.pdf","Untitled document","document1 (2).pptx"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A name with your last name, the course, the assignment, and a version is identifiable and avoids overwrite collisions; 'final.docx' is ambiguous among many submissions.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_meaningless_filename","difficulty":"medium"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_wrong_export_format","pattern":"submit_editable_instead_of_required","description":"Submitting the working file format when the assignment requires a fixed or exported format","trigger":"learner uploads a .pptx or .docx when a PDF was required, or shares an editable link instead of a published one","feedback":"Read the format requirement. Export to the required type (often PDF for layout fidelity) and open the exported file once to confirm it looks right before submitting.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_untested_link","pattern":"assume_link_works","description":"Submitting a GitHub Pages or shared-doc link without opening it in a fresh, signed-out browser","trigger":"learner pastes a link that works on their machine but is private or 404 to others","feedback":"Test the link in an incognito or signed-out window. A page that loads for you may be private, a localhost address, or a 404 for the grader.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_meaningless_filename","pattern":"ambiguous_file_naming","description":"Naming the submission 'final.docx' or 'untitled' so it cannot be identified among many submissions","trigger":"file is named 'final' or 'document1' with no name, course, or assignment","feedback":"Use a clear convention like lastname_cat100_capstone.pdf. A meaningful, consistent name makes the file identifiable and avoids overwrite collisions.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_7_ai_confirms_complete","pattern":"trust_ai_completeness_claim","description":"Treating an AI tool's claim that the submission is complete as verification, rather than running each test yourself.","trigger":"learner accepts an AI 'everything is done' confirmation without opening files or testing links","feedback":"An AI cannot open your exported PDF or load your link signed out. Let it draft the checklist, but verify every row against the real artifact yourself; the recipient's environment is the only test that counts.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"49d04a5545675a4ddc395904f52839f6dbf8b6ec8f4c5c1bd6ba3f9cb50b3aa9","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}